# Assistive Stick for Visually Impaired Navigation

An Arduino-based walking stick that detects obstacles via ultrasonic sensor
and communicates proximity through beep frequency.

**Built:** Grade 9, school engineering team project.
**My role:** Head of Technical Department — wrote the firmware and
coordinated the hardware build.

![Wiring diagram](wiring-diagram.png)

## How it works

- HC-SR04 ultrasonic sensor measures distance to obstacles ahead
- Buzzer and LED trigger when something is within 100 cm
- **Beep frequency scales with distance** — closer object, faster beeps
- Battery voltage monitored via analog pin; low-battery warning over serial

## Components

| Component | Purpose |
|-----------|---------|
| Arduino Uno | Microcontroller |
| HC-SR04 | Ultrasonic distance sensor |
| Piezo buzzer | Audio proximity feedback |
| LED | Visual proximity feedback |
| 6V battery pack | Power |

## Design decision

The core design question wasn't *how to detect obstacles* — the HC-SR04
handles that. It was *how to communicate distance* without overwhelming
the user. A constant beep when something is close is annoying, but a
beep that gets faster as you approach an object gives a continuous sense
of proximity. That's the version we shipped.

## Note on the prototype

The physical prototype was built and demonstrated at a school science fair
in Grade 9, and is stored at the school I no longer attend. The photo above
is a TinkerCAD wiring simulation of the same circuit.

This was a **team project**. I served as Head of Technical Department —
I wrote the firmware and coordinated the hardware build. The physical
assembly was collaborative.

## What I'd improve

- Replace the buzzer with a **vibration motor** — less intrusive in public
- Use a proper lithium battery with a real fuel gauge
- Add a side sensor for better spatial awareness

